ow different the token length distribution between two languages were, I ca=
lculated the </span><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Earth_mover%27=
s_distance" rel=3D"">earth mover=E2=80=99s distance</a><span> (also known a=
s the </span><a href=3D"https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/generate=
d/scipy.stats.wasserstein_distance.html" rel=3D"">Wasserstein distance</a><=
span>) between two distributions. Essentially, this metric calculates the m=
inimum amount of =E2=80=9Cwork=E2=80=9D required to transform one distribut=
ion into another. Larger values mean the distributions are farther apart (m=
ore different) while smaller values mean the distributions are quite simila=
r. </span></p><p>Here is a small subset of languages. Note that the distanc=
e says nothing about the length of the tokens, just how similar the distrib=
ution of token lengths are for two languages. For example, Arabic and Russi=
an have similar distributions even though the languages themselves are not =
similar in a linguistic sense.</p><div class=3D"captioned-image-container">=
